## Everyday commands

```bash
flutter pub get                 # install dependencies
flutter analyze                 # lints and types
dart format .                   # format
flutter test --coverage         # tests (then: dart run tool/check_coverage.dart --min <%coverageMobile%>)
```

`./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc gates` runs the same checks from the repository root, with the coverage floor, and `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc verify` adds the spec's facts. Conventions for agents and people: [`<%mobileRuleFile%>`](../<%mobileRuleFile%>).

## Run it on a device or emulator

The `android/` and `ios/` folders are not generated (they change with every Flutter release). Create them once, with your own reverse-domain `--org`, and commit them:

```bash
flutter create --platforms=android,ios --org com.example --project-name <%dartPackage%> .
flutter run<%#hasBackend%> --dart-define=API_URL=http://10.0.2.2:<%backendPort%><%/hasBackend%>
```
<%#hasBackend%>

`API_URL` is fixed when the app is built (a mobile app has no environment to read): `10.0.2.2` is how an Android emulator reaches the computer it runs on; the iOS simulator can use `http://localhost:<%backendPort%>`, which is also the default. Android blocks plain `http://` in release builds, so a deployed API needs `https://`.
<%/hasBackend%>

Building the APK or the iOS app needs the Android SDK or Xcode, which the gates do not require: run `flutter build apk` or `flutter build ipa` yourself when you release.
